LRC14 FAREY PRODUCT / PERFECT / KURATOWSKI ATLAS
============================================================================

A. Product-set divisor closure
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
P_n = {a*b : 0 < a <= b <= n, gcd(a,b)=1}.
M_n = max(P_n) = n*(n-1), except M_1=1.
D(M_n) is always contained in P_n; equality is the special gate.

 n | |P_n| | M_n | sigma(M_n) | sum(P_n) | excess | closure | perfect tag
 1 |     1 |     1 |          1 |        1 |      0 | True    | -
 2 |     2 |     2 |          3 |        3 |      0 | True    | -
 3 |     4 |     6 |         12 |       12 |      0 | True    | M perfect, half-sum perfect
 4 |     6 |    12 |         28 |       28 |      0 | True    | sum perfect
 5 |    10 |    20 |         42 |       78 |     36 | False   | -
 6 |    11 |    30 |         72 |      108 |     36 | False   | -
 7 |    17 |    42 |         96 |      255 |    159 | False   | -
 8 |    21 |    56 |        120 |      383 |    263 | False   | -
 9 |    27 |    72 |        195 |      626 |    431 | False   | -
10 |    29 |    90 |        234 |      786 |    552 | False   | -
11 |    39 |   110 |        216 |     1391 |   1175 | False   | -
12 |    42 |   132 |        336 |     1667 |   1331 | False   | -
13 |    54 |   156 |        392 |     2681 |   2289 | False   | -
14 |    57 |   182 |        336 |     3143 |   2807 | False   | -
15 |    62 |   210 |        576 |     3938 |   3362 | False   | -

Exact closure proof sketch:
  Every divisor d of n*(n-1) splits as d1*d2 with d1|n, d2|(n-1),
  gcd(d1,d2)=1.  Then d = a*b for a=min(d1,d2), b=max(d1,d2),
  and b <= n, so d is in P_n.
  For n >= 5, (n-2)*(n-1) is in P_n via (n-2)/(n-1),
  but it does not divide n*(n-1), since n-2 does not divide n.
  Thus P_n = D(M_n) only for n <= 4.

B. The F3/F4 perfect gate
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
F_3: P_3=[1, 2, 3, 6], M=6, sigma(M)=12, sum(P)=12
  product  1: 1/1:planar-star
  product  2: 1/2:planar-star
  product  3: 1/3:planar-star
  product  6: 2/3:planar-two-block
F_4: P_4=[1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12], M=12, sigma(M)=28, sum(P)=28
  product  1: 1/1:planar-star
  product  2: 1/2:planar-star
  product  3: 1/3:planar-star
  product  4: 1/4:planar-star
  product  6: 2/3:planar-two-block
  product 12: 3/4:K33-minor

Readout:
  F3 is planar and divisor-closed at M=6; sigma(6)=12=2*6.
  F4 is the last divisor-closed order and sum(P4)=28, the next perfect number.
  The same F4 step introduces 3/4 -> K_{3,4}, which contains K33.

C. Edge-count aliases are not obstruction cores
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
K33 edge count 9               : first at F_9: 1/9->planar-star
K5 edge count 10               : first at F_5: 2/5->planar-two-block
K5 disjoint K33 edge count 19  : first at F_19: 1/19->planar-star
F4 max/divisor gate 12         : first at F_4: 3/4->K33-minor
perfect-number edge alias 28   : first at F_7: 4/7->K33-minor
first actual K33-minor term: F_4, 3/4, product 12

Kuratowski guardrail:
  K5 and K33 are a two-element obstruction set.
  K5 disjoint K33 is nonplanar because it already contains the cores.
  Edge-count aliases such as product 10 or 19 do not create new cores.

D. Mediant/minor noncommutation
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2/3 -> planar-two-block
1/1 -> planar-star
mediant 3/4 -> K33-minor
  Two planar Farey parents can mediant into the first K33-minor child.
  Minor/subdivision containment is transitive; mediant-taking is not that operation.

E. Perfect-sum finite scout
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
F_3: |P|=4, sum(P)=12, half-sum-perfect
F_4: |P|=6, sum(P)=28, sum-perfect
No other perfect or half-perfect product sums occur for n <= 1000.

F. New hypotheses generated by this atlas
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
HYP-2944: F3/F4 form a perfect-product obstruction gate.
HYP-2945: Farey mediants and graph minors do not commute; K33 begins at 3/4.
HYP-2946: After F4, product-excess P_n \ D(n(n-1)) is the divisor-leakage signal.

G. Tournament analysis
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Relation: x -> y iff x retains more proof-critical structure than y.
vertices=8 edges=28 c3=0 hp=1
  score 7: exact LRC M/Farey branch
  score 6: AP/GW C27 shell labels
  score 5: Farey product divisor closure
  score 4: Kpq minor/subdivision obstruction
  score 3: post-F4 product-excess leakage
  score 2: perfect/aliquot shadow
  score 1: raw edge-count aliases
  score 0: mediant as numerical average

Verdict:
  F3 and F4 are not just small examples.  F3 is the planar perfect-max
  closure, and F4 is simultaneously the last divisor closure, the first
  K33-minor Farey product term, and the product-sum 28 perfect-number hit.
  From F5 onward the extra product layer is real leakage, so LRC14 should
  keep exact C27/Farey labels before using product/perfect analogies.
